p = "plan.md"
s = open(p, encoding="utf-8").read()
i = s.index("Proposal for `training/radar_bev.py`")
j = s.index("\n", i)
new = ("""**8. The module and the side loss (Mirko: "use side losses as help first, but they are not the final goal").** Built as `training/radar_encoder.py` (`RadarEncoder`; `radar_bev.py` stays as the simple block): delta-coded slot raster (valid, dr, dpsi, Doppler code, rcs, age, offset, ray origin; K = 3 + remainder), two 3 x 3 convs, LeakyReLU, C = 64 >= 61 inputs, pass-through start. `forward(det, B, with_state=True)` returns the features and the lossless raster (explicit stream). The side loss `aux_loss` (1 x 1 decoder, every field weighted by 1 / its tolerance) is a HELPER of the first phase: `aux_weight(step)` = 1.0 decaying with a half-life to a floor of 0.05; the task losses (segmentation, objects, velocity) are the goal. `kept_fraction` measures, without gradient, how many detections the features still hold, so the effect of the task training on the information is visible in the log instead of assumed. Why the side loss is needed at all, measured with a proxy task (cell holds a moving detection, |vr| > 1 m/s; `tests/test_radar_encoder.py`, 2000 steps, 8 train / 2 held-out scenes, probe = fresh 1 x 1 decoder on the frozen features, default start = a lower bound): task alone F1 0.983 and 21.3 % of the detections kept (oracle 95.0 %); task + decaying side loss F1 0.983 and 77.3 % kept; side loss alone 76.4 % (task F1 0.195). So the side loss costs the proxy task nothing and keeps about three times as much of the cloud. A pass-through-start probe and the module's own decoder are measured in the requeued run (the GPU is booked for the t1 rounds). Not yet shown: the same with the real heads (segmentation / objects / velocity) - the proxy task needs only one field, the real tasks need more; the monitor is built into the module for exactly that run, and the floor of the weight is the knob.
Further proposal for the shared block: the same delta idea could apply to Doppler if an adapter supplies the raw value (predictor = static hypothesis -(v_ego + omega x s) . u, residual ~ 0 for static content); nuScenes already delivers the ego-compensated value, so there the predictor is 0.""")
s = s[:i] + new + s[j:]
open(p, "w", encoding="utf-8").write(s)

p = "tests/README.md"
s = open(p, encoding="utf-8").read()
row = ("| `test_radar_encoder.py` | the MODULE `training/radar_encoder.py` on real nuScenes mini: three trainings (side loss alone, proxy task alone = does the cell hold a moving detection, task + side loss with `aux_weight` 1.0 -> 0.05), each followed by a fresh 1x1 probe on the frozen features (pass-through start) and the kept fraction (all six fields within tolerance) vs the oracle (decode the raster itself); task F1 on held-out scenes | task + side loss keeps >= 0.9 x oracle on held-out scenes and its task F1 is within 0.03 of task alone | first run (default-start probe = lower bound): oracle 95.0 %; side loss alone 76.4 % (F1 0.195); task alone 21.3 % (F1 0.983); task + side loss 77.3 % (F1 0.983) -> gate FAILS on the probe, not shown to fail on the module; rerun with the pass-through probe and the module's own decoder is queued behind the GPU rounds (`run_when_gpu_free.sh`) |\n")
anchor = "| `test_object_head.py`"
s = s.replace(anchor, row + anchor, 1)
open(p, "w", encoding="utf-8").write(s)
print("ok")
